-- title:
--   Theorem 4.1: there is a unique team-wise equilibrium $a^*$, and $0<\mathbb E[G(\sum_ta^*_t)]<\infty$
-- statement:
--   Under the §4 standing assumptions ($N\ge1$; $C_t$ strictly increasing, strictly convex, $C^1$, $C_t(0)=C_t'(0)=0$; $R,G\ge0$ jointly Borel; $\theta\in(0,1)$) and Assumption 4.1, there exists a unique team-wise equilibrium $a^*\in\mathcal A$:
--   $$a^*\in\arg\max_{a\in\mathcal A}\mathbb V(a,a^*),\qquad\mathbb V(a,a^*)=\mathbb E\Big[R\Big(\sum_ta_t\Big)\Big((1-\theta)+\theta\frac{G(\sum_ta_t)}{\mathbb E[G(\sum_ta^*_t)]}\Big)-\sum_tC_t(a_t)\Big],$$
--   and every team-wise equilibrium equals $a^*$ almost surely. Moreover,
--   $$0<\mathbb E\Big[G\Big(\sum_{t=0}^{N-1}a^*_t\Big)\Big]<\infty.$$
--
--   This is the existence and uniqueness result for the top layer of competition between teams; Theorem 4.2 builds the two-layer equilibrium on it.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Team-wise equilibrium (Definition 4.1): $\mathbb V(a^*,a^*)$ is finite and every $a\in\mathcal A$ satisfies $\mathbb V(a,a^*)\le\mathbb V(a^*,a^*)$ in the extended sense. The idiosyncratic noise does not enter.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: "continuously differentiable" for R, G, RG, J → continuous on ℝ₊ and C¹ on (0, ∞) (footnote 2); derivative conditions at x > 0; reason: footnote 2 (p.5) defines this meaning; the paper's Examples 3.1 and 4.1 (R = x^p g(y), p < 1) satisfy its assumptions only in this sense.
--   - encoding: unique team-wise equilibrium → every team-wise equilibrium equals a* almost surely in every period; reason: controls are random variables, determined up to null sets.
--   - encoding: 0 < E[G(Σ a*_t)] < ∞ → G(Σ a*_t, Y) integrable and 0 < ∫ G(Σ a*_t, Y) dμ; reason: nonnegative integrand; a Bochner integral "< ∞" would be vacuous.
--   - encoding: a* ∈ argmax_{a∈𝒜} 𝕍(a, a*) → 𝕍(a*, a*) integrable and every a ∈ 𝒜 has 𝕍(a, a*) ≤ 𝕍(a*, a*) in the extended sense (ExpLE); reason: an argmax of expectations presupposes a finite maximal value; the extended comparison keeps every deviation.
--   - encoding: controls in 𝒜 (resp. 𝒜̃): F^Y- (resp. F^{i,Y}-) adapted, ℝ₊-valued → families a k : Ω → ℝ, nonnegative, measurable for σ of the k-prefix of Y (resp. of X and Y); reason: Doob–Dynkin: the same as the paper's control laws a_k(X_1, …, X_k, Y_1, …, Y_k).
--   - encoding: an N-period model → 0 < N; reason: excludes only the degenerate model N = 0, where all sums are empty and (3.6) is vacuous.
--   - encoding: periods k = 0, …, N − 1 with noises X_{k+1}, Y_{k+1}; F^Y_k = σ(Y_1, …, Y_k) → Lean coordinates X k, Y k (0-based); F^Y_k is generated by the k-prefix of the noise path (truncPath); reason: Lean convention; same instances.
--   - generalization: the standing model of §3/§4 also fixes the idiosyncratic noise X (independent of Y, X_1, …, X_N independent) and φ_k with (3.1) → no assumption on X or φ; reason: X and φ do not occur in the statement, which therefore specializes to the standing model.
--   - encoding: Assumption 4.1(iv)–(v), inequalities between random variables 'for all x ≥ 0' (resp. all x ∈ ℝ₊ᴺ) → almost surely, simultaneously for all x (and t); reason: R, RG are continuous on ℝ₊ by (i), G = RG/R is continuous on (0, ∞) where R > 0 by (ii), and the costs are C¹, so the a.s. inequality for each x and for all x at once coincide; same instances.
-- source:
--   Zhou, Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution games, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), Theorem 4.1, p.19; (4.1) and Definition 4.1, pp.18–19

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_Setup
import Definitions.Def_RelPerfMFG_TwoLayer

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace RelPerfMFG

/-- **Theorem 4.1**, Zhou, *Optimal relative performance criteria in mean field contribution
games*, SSRN 3742584 (Nov. 13, 2022), p.19. Under Assumption 4.1, there exists a unique team-wise
equilibrium `a* ∈ 𝒜`. Moreover, `0 < E[G(Σ_{t=0}^{N−1} a*_t)] < ∞`.

**Formalization Note.** §4 standing assumptions: `(Ω, μ)` a probability space, `Y` the
common-noise path, `C_t` strictly increasing, strictly convex, `C¹`, `C_t(0) = C′_t(0) = 0`,
`R, G ≥ 0` jointly Borel, `θ ∈ (0, 1)`, `N ≥ 1`. Team-wise equilibrium is Definition 4.1
(`IsTeamwiseEq`, payoff comparisons in the extended sense). Uniqueness is up to a.s. equality:
every team-wise equilibrium agrees with `a*` a.s. in every period. `E[G(Σ a*_t)] < ∞` is
integrability. The noise `X` does not enter. 0-based periods. -/
theorem theorem_4_1 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {N e : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N)
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (Y : Ω → NoisePath N e) (hY : Measurable Y)
    (C : Fin N → ℝ → ℝ) (hC : IsCostS4 C)
    (R G : ℝ → NoisePath N e → ℝ) (hR : IsReward R) (hG : IsReward G)
    (θ : ℝ) (hθ : θ ∈ Set.Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1) (hA : Assumption41 μ Y C R G) :
    ∃ aS : Fin N → Ω → ℝ, IsTeamwiseEq μ Y C R G θ aS ∧
      (∀ a : Fin N → Ω → ℝ, IsTeamwiseEq μ Y C R G θ a → ∀ t, a t =ᵐ[μ] aS t) ∧
      Integrable (fun ω => G (∑ t, aS t ω) (Y ω)) μ ∧
      0 < ∫ ω, G (∑ t, aS t ω) (Y ω) ∂μ := by sorry

end RelPerfMFG
